functions.doc:
Run the benchmark (see the module docstring). Re-running with the same ``out_dir`` resumes from the cache;
a different configuration is refused unless ``force``. Missing artifacts are prepared first
(``prepare_missing``); stale or failed ones stop the run unless ``allow_failed_validation``.

Budget study and ablations (setup audit), from the run files.

{pid: {"study": {mid: {suffix: [{"budget_s", "metrics", "source"}, ...]}},
       "ablation": {mid: {"case", "baseline": metrics, "variants": {name: metrics}}}}}

Train on ``n_sims`` pairs ``z ~ prior`` (uniform on [0, 1]^d by default), ``y = noise(simulate(z))``;
10 % of them are held out to keep the best checkpoint. ``threads``: torch threads while training (small nets
train fastest on one).

classes.doc:
Wraps a value function v: State -> float and a baseline state;
`attribute(causes)` returns the exact Shapley decomposition of
v(all-current) - v(all-baseline) across the given causes.

Base class. Subclasses set ``name``, ``inputs`` (input column names), ``outputs`` (observable names),
``parameters`` (defaults) and implement ``predict``.

Optional capabilities, detected by the methods that use them:

* ``predict_torch(theta: dict[str, Tensor], inputs, observable) -> Tensor``: exact autograd Jacobians/gradients;
* ``sensitivities(theta, obs, names) -> (prediction, dprediction/dtheta)``: the model's own derivatives (forward
  or adjoint sensitivities of a discretised PDE), in physical units;
* ``adjoint_gradient(theta, obs, names) -> (misfit, dmisfit/dtheta)``: gradient of
  ``0.5 * sum(((pred - values) / sigma)**2)`` by one adjoint solve;
* ``fields(theta) -> dict``: reconstructed physical fields (``{"name": {"x": [...], "values": [...], "unit"}}``);
* ``pinn(obs, parameters, **opts) -> dict``: a physics-informed network for this model.

``cost``: ``"cheap"`` (milliseconds: MCMC and profile likelihood run by default), ``"moderate"`` (tens of
milliseconds: profile likelihood, no MCMC) or ``"expensive"`` (neither, unless asked).

A model written as text, e.g. ``"T_air + (T0 - T_air) * exp(-h * t / tau_c)"``, one expression per observable.

Parsed safely (``pinneapple_mcp.expressions``: numbers, names, arithmetic and a fixed list of NumPy functions;
nothing is passed to ``eval``). Names must be parameters or inputs.

children.doc:
Ready-made inverse problems with known answers, and models described as JSON (for apps and agents).

Every preset's measurements are *synthetic*: the forward model at the stated true parameters plus Gaussian noise
from a fixed seed, so any estimate can be checked against the truth (``Parameter.truth``). The heat cases are the
Inverse Heat Lab's (pin fin and heat-spreader plate), plus a fin with zone-wise convection (a field), an unknown
base temperature (a boundary condition), a cooling curve and a reaction-kinetics case.

``model_from_spec`` builds a model from JSON::

    {"type": "pin_fin", "options": {"d_mm": 5, "length_mm": 50}}
    {"type": "expression", "expressions": {"T": "T_air + (T0 - T_air) * exp(-k * t)"}, "inputs": ["t"],
     "parameters": [{"name": "k", "value": 0.01, "lower": 1e-5, "upper": 1}, ...]}
    {"type": "table", "rows": [{...}, ...], "parameters": ["h"], "inputs": ["x_mm"], "output": "T"}

functions.doc:
Per input: ``freeze`` when the upper bound of the total-index interval is below ``threshold`` for every output;
``inconclusive`` when the estimate is below but the interval is not (more samples needed); ``keep`` otherwise.
Evidence lists the total index and interval per output (and Morris ``mu_star`` when given).

children.doc:
One model interface for uncertainty and sensitivity studies: ``predict(X) -> (n, m)`` with input/output names,
units and validity range, whatever the model is.

- :meth:`StudyModel.from_zoo`: a ``pp.zoo`` model (torch); exposes the physical-inputs torch module for automatic
  differentiation, the ensemble spread when the model has one, its validity range and its reference dataset splits;
- :meth:`StudyModel.from_onnx`: an ONNX graph (no Python code from the user is run): one ``(n, d)`` float input,
  outputs concatenated in graph order; needs ``onnxruntime``;
- :meth:`StudyModel.from_function`: any vectorised Python callable (the built-in benchmark functions, a closed-form
  model, a solver wrapper);
- :func:`builtin`: the analytic benchmarks of :mod:`pinneapple_analysis.sensitivity.testfunctions`.

classes.doc:
``X`` (n, d) inputs; ``Y`` (n, m) scalar outputs or (n, *grid_shape) fields.

``coords`` (n_points, n_dims) are the field's grid coordinates (fields only), ``coord_names`` their names;
``groups`` (n,) an optional group label per sample (a geometry or a case), used for group-held-out splits;
``meta`` free-form provenance (source, solver, cost per sample in ``solver_s_per_sample``).

methods.doc:
``{"outputs": {name: list}, "ood": [per-row assessment], "n": rows}``. ``ood_policy``: ``"warn"``
predicts every row and flags the out-of-distribution ones; ``"refuse"`` returns ``None`` for them;
``"ignore"`` skips the gate (rows are still assessed and reported).

functions.doc:
Forward: predictions at ``query``. Inverse: the ``unknown`` inputs (inside the model's bounds) that make the
model reproduce ``observed`` outputs with the ``known`` inputs, by bounded least squares from several starts;
then the estimate is re-run through ``reference`` when given.

children.doc:
Train / validation / held-out test / distribution-shift splits for surrogate studies.

The distribution-shift split is carved out first and never seen in training, so its error is a measured
extrapolation error, not an estimate:

``"group"``     whole groups held out (a geometry, a case, a machine never seen in training);
``"edge"``      the samples farthest from the centre of the input space (normalised distance), i.e. the boundary of
                the design space, while training covers the interior;
``{"column": name, "side": "high" | "low"}``  the top or bottom of one input (extrapolation along that parameter);
``"auto"``      ``data.meta["shift"]`` when the dataset recommends one, else ``"group"`` when the data has groups,
                else ``"edge"``; ``None`` for no shift split.

The rest is split at random into train, validation (model selection and early stopping only) and the held-out test.

functions.doc:
See the module docstring. ``budget``: a ``ComputeBudget``, a dict or seconds of wall clock per training run;
``seeds``: a count or explicit seeds; ``pde``: a declaration (``pde.py``) or ``data.meta['pde']``;
``target``: the held-out error a deployable model must reach (default 0.10 nRMSE, 0.05 relative L2).

children.doc:
Uncertain inputs of a study: one distribution per parameter, sampled by Monte Carlo, Latin hypercube or Sobol.

A parameter is a dict (or anything with ``to_dict()``) such as::

    {"name": "k", "dist": "uniform", "low": 150, "high": 400, "unit": "W/(m K)"}
    {"name": "T", "dist": "normal", "mean": 25, "std": 2}
    {"name": "V", "dist": "triangular", "low": 0.5, "mode": 2, "high": 6}
    {"name": "h", "dist": "lognormal", "median": 30, "gsd": 1.2}       # geometric standard deviation
    {"name": "t", "dist": "truncnormal", "mean": 2, "std": 0.3, "low": 1.5, "high": 2.5}

or a ``{name: (low, high)}`` mapping (uniform on each range). Every distribution is sampled through its inverse CDF
from a point of the unit hypercube, so the same design (QMC, LHS, Saltelli, Morris, polynomial chaos) serves every
distribution. Used by :mod:`pinneapple_analysis.sensitivity` and the propagation in
:func:`pinneapple_analysis.uncertainty.tolerance.propagate_space`.

classes.doc:
``apply(problem, case_or_dataset, magnitude, rng)``. Model faults return ``(faulty_case, correct_field)``;
data faults return ``(faulty_dataset, worst relative error of a corrupted sample)`` (0 when the samples are
right but the dataset is not: duplicates, leakage).

children.doc:
The detection matrix: every check against every fault and magnitude, and against clean cases.

``run_fault_injection(problem)`` draws correct cases from the problem's verified solver (with the variation a
threshold must tolerate: coefficient contrast, feature size, amplitude, solver resolution), injects every fault at
every magnitude and records each check's value. From the values:

* the detection rate of each check (and of all checks together) per fault and magnitude;
* the false-alarm rate of each check on the clean cases;
* the minimal magnitude each check detects (detection rate >= ``min_rate``, default 90 %);
* the ROC AUC of each check per fault and magnitude (clean values against faulty values);
* the true error each fault causes (relative L2 against the correct field).

The report renders as Markdown (the "check power" section of a validation report) and as JSON.

functions.doc:
Relations of a tabular model from a declarative list (TOML/JSON), e.g.::

{kind = "monotonic", input = "air_velocity_m_s", output = "r_base_k_w", direction = "decreasing"}
{kind = "invariant", inputs = {power_w = "2 * power_w"}, output = "r_base_k_w", tol = 0.02}
{kind = "symmetric", inputs = {M0 = "-M0", alpha_deg = "-alpha_deg"}, outputs = {Cl = "-Cl"}}

Validate ``model`` on ``problem``.

``data``: ``{"train": {...}, "test": {...}}`` (Darcy: arrays ``a`` and ``u`` of shape ``(n, ny, nx)``);
``reference``: ``(inputs, outputs)`` pairs to compare with (instead of ``data["test"]``); ``query``: the inputs
the model will be used on, checked without ground truth (validity range, residual, calibrated bound);
``method``: the model's catalog id (``pinneapple_catalog``) for code verification.
